<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Wenhu Chen, Wei Lu, Tianyu Pang<span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF0040;">*</span>
</span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF0040;">*</span>Equal contribution. $ˆ<span id="id3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">‡</span>$Correspondence to Tianyu Pang.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>NTU
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Waterloo
<span id="id4" class="ltx_inline-block fa fa-github" aria-hidden="true">
</span>  <a href="https://github.com/sail-sg/feedback-conditional-policy" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_bold">Code Link</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs are often trained with RL from human or AI feedback, yet such methods typically <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compress nuanced feedback into scalar rewards</em>, discarding much of their richness and inducing scale imbalance. We propose treating verbal feedback as a conditioning signal. Inspired by language priors in text-to-image generation, which enable novel outputs from unseen prompts, we introduce the <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">feedback-conditional policy (FCP)</span>. FCP learns directly from response-feedback pairs, approximating the feedback-conditional posterior through maximum likelihood training on <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">offline</em> data. We further develop an <em id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">online bootstrapping</em> stage where the policy generates under positive conditions and receives fresh feedback to refine itself. This reframes feedback-driven learning as conditional generation rather than reward optimization, offering a more expressive way for LLMs to directly learn from verbal feedback.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<blockquote id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“That all of what we mean by goals and purposes can be well thought of as maximization of the expected value of the cumulative sum of a received scalar signal (reward).”</em>  — Reward Hypothesis by Richard Sutton</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reward hypothesis</em> in reinforcement learning (RL) was proposed over two decades ago <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton, 2004</a>)</cite>, when feedback from the environment had to be reduced to <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">scalar rewards</span> for RL algorithms to operate. This view shaped much of the field’s progress and remains the prevailing standard in applying RL to <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">alignment</em> and <em id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reasoning</em> for large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziegler et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Yet in practice, the feedback encountered in RL for LLMs, especially in <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-verifiable</em> settings, is most often <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">verbalized</span>, such as “<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Good start, but the code can be more efficient</span>”. Such feedback may come from human users <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stephan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, generative reward models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, or tool outputs in agentic scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Reducing the verbal feedback into scalar rewards introduces several limitations:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I.</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Information loss.</span> Scalar rewards capture far less information than verbal feedback/critiques and are often uninterpretable. For example, the critiques “<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">The response is redundant but correct</span>” and “<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">The response is compact but has many typos</span>” may both collapse to a reward of <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math>, despite describing very different response patterns. Furthermore, the verbalized thoughts produced by (generative) reward models are typically discarded as intermediate outputs, with only the final scalar retained for RL training.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">II.</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ambiguity.</span> Verbal feedback, especially from human users, is often <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mixed</em> (containing both pros and cons), <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">emotional</em>, or <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uncertain</em>, such as “<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">I’m so happy</span>” or “<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">I’m not sure, maybe try again?</span>”. Such feedback is far more common than purely positive or negative signals and carries diverse cues for learning and for understanding user interaction styles. Mapping these forms of feedback to scalars could be unclear or arbitrary.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">III.</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Imbalanced reward scales across tasks.</span> In multi-task training (e.g., math, code, science, games), it is difficult to maintain a consistent reward scale. Positive feedback on a simple math problem is far easier to obtain than on a challenging coding or game-playing task, which induces imbalanced rewards across domains and biases the learning process.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Scalarization has long been seen as unavoidable, bridging verbal feedback and the numerical signals required by RL. With the rise of large-scale language pretraining, however, this view is being re-examined <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao, 2025</a>)</cite>. LLMs embody strong commonsense and linguistic <span id="S1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">priors</span>, suggesting a new paradigm: <em id="S1.p3.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">treat verbal feedback as a first-class training signal</em>, rather than forcing it into a scalar form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">After all, LLMs already show the ability to <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">implicitly understand verbal feedback</span>. In agentic tasks, they iteratively adapt by integrating feedback prompts from human users, external critiques, or tool calls into their context and refining their responses accordingly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Novikov et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. This indicates that LLMs can process verbal feedback, but only implicitly, <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">through a latent “mental model” that does not convert understanding into explicit scalar rewards</em>. The key question, then, is how to distill such feedback into training so that it directly improves model performance, rather than relying on inefficient multi-turn trial and error at test time.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.22638v1/verbal_demo.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/185;" width="548" height="185" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning from mixed verbal feedback.</span> The instruction <math id="S1.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> is “<em id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Write a Python function flatten(lst) that returns a flat list of integers</em>”. The reference policy <math id="S1.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> may assign low probability to the ideal response <math id="S1.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, making purely positive response-feedback pairs <math id="S1.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{o}}^{+},{\bm{c}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{o}}^{+},{\bm{c}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> rare in the training data collected from <math id="S1.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{env}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This resembles the setting of text-to-image generation, where the <em id="S1.F1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">language prior</em> enables models to combine seen captions (analogous to mixed feedback <math id="S1.F1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒄</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.F1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒄</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) and generate rare images (analogous to purely positive response <math id="S1.F1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>) such as “<em id="S1.F1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a banana surfing on the ocean</em>” (Figure <a href="#A0.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Motivated by this, our model <math id="S1.F1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained as a feedback-conditional policy (FCP), and when conditioning on user-defined positive <math id="S1.F1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is <math id="S1.F1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\propto\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\propto\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we propose to learn a <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">feedback-conditional policy (FCP)</span> <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\propto\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\propto\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a reference policy that generates a response <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> given an instruction <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the distribution of environment feedback <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Intuitively, the FCP reweighs the reference policy by how likely each response <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> would elicit the observed feedback <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conditioning on positive feedback <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\propto\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\propto\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which increases the probability of generating responses that are more likely to receive favorable feedback. In this way, the FCP learns a <em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">posterior</em> distribution that integrates prior knowledge from <math id="S1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> with verbal feedback, allowing it to handle diverse forms of feedback, including mixed ones, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">After training an <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">offline</em> FCP <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\propto\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\propto\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math> that conditions on arbitrary feedback <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we further improve it through <em id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">online bootstrapping</em>. Concretely, we conduct online training by sampling rollouts from the behavior policy <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> (goal-conditioned on positive feedback), and re-annotating them with fresh feedback from <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{env}}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby iteratively strengthening the policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our pilot experiments show that FCP matches or surpasses strong scalar-based baselines such as offline RFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and online GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">without relying on verifiers, scalar conversion, or data filtering</span>. This demonstrates a simple and scalable framework that preserves the richness of verbal feedback while avoiding the scarcity of rule-based verifiers and the risk of reward hacking. While our current implementation is naive, advanced training techniques could further improve FCP’s performance. Due to space constraints, related work is deferred to Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Related work ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Learning directly from verbal feedback</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional RL methods train a policy by up-weighting responses that receive “good” feedback and down-weighting those that receive “bad” feedback. From a probabilistic view, RL can be seen as learning a <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">posterior</em> over responses that are expected to receive good feedback (i.e., high rewards) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peters &amp; Schaal, 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Distinguishing what counts as good or bad typically requires carefully designed reward functions or detailed rubrics to produce scalar signals, leading to the limitations discussed in Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach is inspired by language priors in text-to-image generation, where models compose <em id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unseen</em> prompts from mixed captions (Figure <a href="#A0.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Similarly, language priors could enable LLMs to absorb diverse verbal feedback and yield high-quality responses beyond scalar reinforcement (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Since LLMs already show implicit feedback understanding, we train directly on it: <em id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">offline</em> to initialize a <span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">feedback-conditional policy (FCP)</span> (Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Offline training: Initializing feedback-conditional policy ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>), then <em id="S2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">online</em> to bootstrap performance (Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Online training: Bootstrapping by conditioning on positive feedback ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Offline training: Initializing feedback-conditional policy</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with a reference policy model <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> that takes an input instruction <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and generates a response <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{o}}\sim\pi_{\textrm{ref}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}\sim\pi_{\textrm{ref}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The response <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> then undergoes a <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single-turn</em> interaction with the environment, which provides verbal feedback <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{c}}\sim p_{\textrm{env}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}\sim p_{\textrm{env}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The reference policy <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> may represent a base model, an instruction-tuned model, or a reasoning model, and the response <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> can include both thinking processes and the final answer. The environment <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{env}}</annotation></semantics></math> may consist of human users or generative reward models. In the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">offline</span> setting, where responses are collected from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the joint distribution of response-feedback pairs as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}},{\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})\triangleq\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}},{\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})\triangleq\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>, from which we derive the <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">feedback-conditional posterior</em> distribution:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})=\frac{P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}},{\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}{P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}=\frac{\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})}{\sum_{\bm{o}}\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})}\textrm{.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})=\frac{P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}},{\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}{P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}=\frac{\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})}{\sum_{\bm{o}}\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})}\textrm{.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Informally, let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> denote purely positive feedback and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> purely negative one. Mixed feedback can be approximated as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}=({\bm{c}}^{+},{\bm{c}}^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}=({\bm{c}}^{+},{\bm{c}}^{-})</annotation></semantics></math>, while neutral or uncertain feedback may be neither. If we condition on positive feedback <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, for instance, “<span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">The generated code is functionally correct, efficient, and compact</span>” for a coding instruction <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\propto\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\propto\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which favors responses <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> that are more likely to elicit positive feedback.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Conditioning on negative feedback <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> would similarly favor poor responses, though this is rarely useful.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\propto\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\propto\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math> appears to be the oracle policy we are seeking, it cannot be directly sampled from, because <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined only after the full response <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated, and thus cannot guide generation step by step. We therefore aim to learn a policy that approximates <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. (2023)</a></cite>, we show that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> is the optimal solution to a KL-constrained reward maximization problem with reward function <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.E2.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="20.23" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-7.83px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 480.96 20.23" width="480.96"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,20.23) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 16.08 C 0 18.37 1.86 20.23 4.15 20.23 L 476.81 20.23 C 479.1 20.23 480.96 18.37 480.96 16.08 L 480.96 4.15 C 480.96 1.86 479.1 0 476.81 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#EDEDED;" fill="#EDEDED" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 16.08 C 0 18.37 1.86 20.23 4.15 20.23 L 476.81 20.23 C 479.1 20.23 480.96 18.37 480.96 16.08 L 480.96 4.15 C 480.96 1.86 479.1 0 476.81 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 2.77 7.83)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:34.36em;--ltx-fo-height:0.8em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.47em;font-size:10pt;" height="17.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.02)" width="475.43"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.E2.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\in\arg\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})}\left[\log p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})\right]-\mathbb{D}_{\textrm{KL}}\left(\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})||\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\right)\textrm{.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">off</mtext></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">arg</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">max</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">[</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">log</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">env</mtext></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">]</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔻</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\in\arg\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})}\left[\log p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})\right]-\mathbb{D}_{\textrm{KL}}\left(\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})||\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\right)\textrm{.}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In the special case where the environment provides <em id="S2.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">verifiable rewards</em>, that is, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}}^{+})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}}^{+})=1</annotation></semantics></math> for correct responses <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}}^{-})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}}^{-})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for incorrect responses <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{o}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can show that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to the optimal solution of a 0-1 binary reward maximization problem without KL regularization: <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="20.23" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-7.83px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 276.17 20.23" width="276.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,20.23) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 16.08 C 0 18.37 1.86 20.23 4.15 20.23 L 272.02 20.23 C 274.31 20.23 276.17 18.37 276.17 16.08 L 276.17 4.15 C 276.17 1.86 274.31 0 272.02 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#EDEDED;" fill="#EDEDED" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 16.08 C 0 18.37 1.86 20.23 4.15 20.23 L 272.02 20.23 C 274.31 20.23 276.17 18.37 276.17 16.08 L 276.17 4.15 C 276.17 1.86 274.31 0 272.02 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 2.77 7.83)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:19.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0.8em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.47em;font-size:10pt;" height="17.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.02)" width="270.64"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.SS1.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\in\arg\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})}\left[\mathds{1}({\bm{o}}\textrm{ is }{\bm{o}}^{+})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">max</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">[</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"> is </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\in\arg\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})}\left[\mathds{1}({\bm{o}}\textrm{ is }{\bm{o}}^{+})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> (proof is in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Proof of Eq. () and its special case ‣ Appendix A Additional derivations and discussions ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alternative learning objective.</span> In more general scenarios, particularly when feedback comes from human users, <em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">solving Eq. (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 Offline training: Initializing feedback-conditional policy ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is typically intractable</em>. This is because we can only sample from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{env}}</annotation></semantics></math> but cannot compute the exact log-likelihood <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the objective in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 Offline training: Initializing feedback-conditional policy ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is equivalent to minimizing the <em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reverse</em> KL divergence between <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{\pi}\!\!\left[\log p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})\right]-\mathbb{D}_{\textrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})||\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\right)\Leftrightarrow\min_{\pi}\mathbb{D}_{\textrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})||P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\right)\!\textrm{,}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo>)</mo></mpadded></mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{\pi}\!\!\left[\log p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})\right]-\mathbb{D}_{\textrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})||\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\right)\Leftrightarrow\min_{\pi}\mathbb{D}_{\textrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})||P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\right)\!\textrm{,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which is derived in Eq. (<a href="#A1.E7" title="In A.1 Proof of Eq. () and its special case ‣ Appendix A Additional derivations and discussions ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). To avoid intractability of computing <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math> in the reverse KL divergence, we instead propose to minimize the <em id="S2.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">forward</em> KL divergence between <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, however, we can only obtain feedback from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is infeasible to sample exclusively from the constrained subset of positive feedback <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math> without carefully designed rubrics or filtering. To address this, we generalize the objective: rather than approximating only <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>, we learn to approximate <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})</annotation></semantics></math>, conditioning directly on <em id="S2.SS1.p3.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any</em> feedback <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we propose to learn a <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">feedback-conditional policy (FCP)</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing the expected forward KL divergence between <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\min_{\pi_{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathbb{D}_{\textrm{KL}}(P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})||\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}))\right]\Leftrightarrow&amp;\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathbb{E}_{P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})}\left[\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\right]\right]\\
\Leftrightarrow&amp;\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathbb{E}_{p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})}\left[\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\right]\right]\textrm{,}\\
\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>min</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>𝔻</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>]</mo><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\min_{\pi_{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathbb{D}_{\textrm{KL}}(P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})||\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}))\right]\Leftrightarrow&amp;\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathbb{E}_{P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})}\left[\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\right]\right]\\
\Leftrightarrow&amp;\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathbb{E}_{p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})}\left[\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\right]\right]\textrm{,}\\
\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where the second equivalence follows from the identities <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})=P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}},{\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})=\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})=P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}},{\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})=\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>. This objective in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Offline training: Initializing feedback-conditional policy ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) reduces to maximum likelihood training, which is straightforward to implement and optimize with data collected from <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>, as described in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.1 Offline training: Initializing feedback-conditional policy ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Its optimal solution is <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS1.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="16.6" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-4.84px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 147.63 16.6" width="147.63"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,16.6) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 12.45 C 0 14.75 1.86 16.6 4.15 16.6 L 143.48 16.6 C 145.77 16.6 147.63 14.75 147.63 12.45 L 147.63 4.15 C 147.63 1.86 145.77 0 143.48 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#EDEDED;" fill="#EDEDED" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 12.45 C 0 14.75 1.86 16.6 4.15 16.6 L 143.48 16.6 C 145.77 16.6 147.63 14.75 147.63 12.45 L 147.63 4.15 C 147.63 1.86 145.77 0 143.48 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 2.77 4.84)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:10.27em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="142.1"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.SS1.p4.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}^{*}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})=P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}^{*}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})=P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> on the support set of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, our approach does not require explicitly distinguishing positive <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> from negative <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>; the language prior embedded in LLMs can implicitly interpret and combine information from diverse forms of feedback, including mixed ones as seen in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. At test time, users may specify desired positive feedback <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and responses can be generated from <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark I: why using <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>?</span> In Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Offline training: Initializing feedback-conditional policy ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), the expectation on <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> is taken w.r.t. <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. In principle, any other distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> could be used, and the optimal solution <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}^{*}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})=P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}^{*}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})=P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})</annotation></semantics></math> would remain unchanged on the support <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{supp}(p(\cdot|{\bm{x}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{supp}(p(\cdot|{\bm{x}}))</annotation></semantics></math>. We adopt <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> mainly for two reasons: <span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(i)</span> its support set <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{supp}(P_{\textrm{off}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}}))=\bigcup_{{\bm{o}}\in\operatorname{supp}(\pi_{\textrm{ref}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}}))}\operatorname{supp}(p_{\textrm{env}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>supp</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mi>supp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{supp}(P_{\textrm{off}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}}))=\bigcup_{{\bm{o}}\in\operatorname{supp}(\pi_{\textrm{ref}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}}))}\operatorname{supp}(p_{\textrm{env}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}}))</annotation></semantics></math> covers all feedback that may be encountered when collecting offline data; <span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(ii)</span> it serves as a compensating distribution that converts the intractable posterior expectation <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})</annotation></semantics></math> into the tractable joint expectation <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}},{\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})=\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{off}}({\bm{o}},{\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})=\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is convenient to sample from.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark II: FCP as inverse dynamics.</span> We observe that our FCP learning in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Offline training: Initializing feedback-conditional policy ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) aligns with modeling <em id="S2.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inverse dynamics</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brandfonbrener et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, complementing supervised finetuning (SFT) as <em id="S2.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">behavior cloning</em>, and critique finetuning (CFT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> as <em id="S2.SS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">forward dynamics</em>. A detailed discussion of this analogy is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Connection to inverse dynamics modeling ‣ Appendix A Additional derivations and discussions ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Offline training: Initializing feedback-conditional policy (Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Offline training: Initializing feedback-conditional policy ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>)</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
            Reference policy <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\textrm{ref}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, feedback environment <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>, feedback-conditional policy <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})</annotation></semantics></math>, instruction corpus <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, optimizer <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
            The offline-trained parameters <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\textrm{off}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\textrm{off}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialize</span> <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="alg1.l3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="19.37" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-5.53px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 233.79 19.37" width="233.79"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,19.37) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 15.22 C 0 17.51 1.86 19.37 4.15 19.37 L 229.63 19.37 C 231.93 19.37 233.79 17.51 233.79 15.22 L 233.79 4.15 C 233.79 1.86 231.93 0 229.63 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#ADD8E6;" fill="#ADD8E6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 15.22 C 0 17.51 1.86 19.37 4.15 19.37 L 229.63 19.37 C 231.93 19.37 233.79 17.51 233.79 15.22 L 233.79 4.15 C 233.79 1.86 231.93 0 229.63 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 2.77 5.53)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:16.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.9em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.3em;font-size:10pt;" height="16.6" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 12.45)" width="228.25"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="alg1.l3.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})=\pi_{\textrm{ref}}\left({\bm{o}}\big|\big[\texttt{&lt;EF&gt;}{\bm{c}}\texttt{&lt;/EF&gt;},{\bm{x}}\big]\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;EF&gt;</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;/EF&gt;</mtext></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})=\pi_{\textrm{ref}}\left({\bm{o}}\big|\big[\texttt{&lt;EF&gt;}{\bm{c}}\texttt{&lt;/EF&gt;},{\bm{x}}\big]\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>, where <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;EF&gt;</span> and <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;/EF&gt;</span> are special tokens used to wrap the expected feedback <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is concatenated before the instruction <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Collect</span> offline dataset <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\textrm{off}}=\{({\bm{x}},{\bm{o}},{\bm{c}})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\textrm{off}}=\{({\bm{x}},{\bm{o}},{\bm{c}})\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\!\sim\!\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\!\sim\!\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{o}}\!\sim\!\pi_{\textrm{ref}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}\!\sim\!\pi_{\textrm{ref}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="alg1.l4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{c}}\!\sim\!p_{\textrm{env}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}\!\sim\!p_{\textrm{env}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective:</span> <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="alg1.l5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="18.06" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-6.3px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 189.71 18.06" width="189.71"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,18.06) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 13.91 C 0 16.21 1.86 18.06 4.15 18.06 L 185.55 18.06 C 187.85 18.06 189.71 16.21 189.71 13.91 L 189.71 4.15 C 189.71 1.86 187.85 0 185.55 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#ADD8E6;" fill="#ADD8E6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 13.91 C 0 16.21 1.86 18.06 4.15 18.06 L 185.55 18.06 C 187.85 18.06 189.71 16.21 189.71 13.91 L 189.71 4.15 C 189.71 1.86 187.85 0 185.55 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 2.77 6.3)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:13.31em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.36em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="184.17"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="alg1.l5.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{({\bm{x}},{\bm{o}},{\bm{c}})\sim\mathcal{D}_{\textrm{off}}}\left[\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">max</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒟</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">off</mtext></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{({\bm{x}},{\bm{o}},{\bm{c}})\sim\mathcal{D}_{\textrm{off}}}\left[\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><span id="alg1.l5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">// Taking expectation over <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\!\sim\!\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" lspace="0.108em" mathcolor="#808080" rspace="0.108em">∼</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#808080">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\!\sim\!\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Offline training: Initializing feedback-conditional policy ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)</span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> not converged <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              
              Sample <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{o}}_{i},{\bm{c}}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{B}\!\sim\!\mathcal{D}_{\textrm{off}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒄</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{o}}_{i},{\bm{c}}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{B}\!\sim\!\mathcal{D}_{\textrm{off}}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\leftarrow\mathcal{O}\mathrm{.step}\big(\theta,\nabla_{\theta}\tfrac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}_{i}|{\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{c}}_{i})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>step</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒄</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\leftarrow\mathcal{O}\mathrm{.step}\big(\theta,\nabla_{\theta}\tfrac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\log\pi_{\theta}({\bm{o}}_{i}|{\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{c}}_{i})\big)</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\textrm{off}}\leftarrow\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\textrm{off}}\leftarrow\theta</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Online training: Bootstrapping by conditioning on positive feedback</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the model obtained by solving the offline problem in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Offline training: Initializing feedback-conditional policy ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\textrm{off}}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\textrm{off}}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is capable of generating responses conditioned on any user-defined feedback <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Building on this model, we further perform <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">online training</span> to bootstrap performance by <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">conditioning explicitly on positive feedback</em> <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. Concretely, we iteratively update parameters <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> using rollouts from <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}=\theta_{\textrm{off}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}=\theta_{\textrm{off}}</annotation></semantics></math> initialized from the offline solution, as described in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 2.2 Online training: Bootstrapping by conditioning on positive feedback ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, we define the joint distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}},{\bm{c}},{\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})\triangleq p_{\textrm{user}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})\cdot\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}},{\bm{c}},{\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})\triangleq p_{\textrm{user}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})\cdot\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{user}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{user}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the distribution (fixed or trainable) of user-specified <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">expected</em> positive feedback. The corresponding feedback-conditional posterior is</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})=\frac{P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}},{\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}{P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}=\frac{\sum_{{\bm{c}}^{+}}p_{\textrm{user}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})\cdot\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})}{\sum_{\bm{o}}\sum_{{\bm{c}}^{+}}p_{\textrm{user}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})\cdot\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})}\textrm{.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})=\frac{P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}},{\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}{P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}=\frac{\sum_{{\bm{c}}^{+}}p_{\textrm{user}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})\cdot\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})}{\sum_{\bm{o}}\sum_{{\bm{c}}^{+}}p_{\textrm{user}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})\cdot\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})\cdot p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})}\textrm{.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The optimization objective for updating <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> based on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (with gradients stopped through <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) is</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\min_{\pi_{\theta_{t+1}}}\mathbb{E}_{P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathbb{D}_{\textrm{KL}}(P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})||\pi_{\theta_{t+1}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}))\right]\\
\Leftrightarrow&amp;\max_{\pi_{\theta_{t+1}}}\mathbb{E}_{P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathbb{E}_{P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})}\left[\log\pi_{\theta_{t+1}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\right]\right]\\
\Leftrightarrow&amp;\max_{\pi_{\theta_{t+1}}}\mathbb{E}_{p_{\textrm{user}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})}\left[\mathbb{E}_{p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})}\left[\log\pi_{\theta_{t+1}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\right]\right]\right]\textrm{.}\\
\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>min</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>𝔻</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\min_{\pi_{\theta_{t+1}}}\mathbb{E}_{P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathbb{D}_{\textrm{KL}}(P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})||\pi_{\theta_{t+1}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}))\right]\\
\Leftrightarrow&amp;\max_{\pi_{\theta_{t+1}}}\mathbb{E}_{P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathbb{E}_{P_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})}\left[\log\pi_{\theta_{t+1}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\right]\right]\\
\Leftrightarrow&amp;\max_{\pi_{\theta_{t+1}}}\mathbb{E}_{p_{\textrm{user}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})}\left[\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})}\left[\mathbb{E}_{p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})}\left[\log\pi_{\theta_{t+1}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\right]\right]\right]\textrm{.}\\
\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intuition.</span> In each training round <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (distinct from the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>-th gradient steps taken within a round), the current model <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> is conditioned on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> to sample candidate positive responses. These responses are then re-annotated with fresh feedback <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the environment. Over successive rounds, the model learns to identify cases where conditioning on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> does not in fact yield positive critiques, while reinforcing those that align with the expected feedback. This iterative process bootstraps the model, progressively strengthening alignment with user-specified positive feedback. Moreover, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lanchantin et al. (2025)</a></cite>, the number of gradient steps <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> between rounds can be flexibly adjusted, allowing the procedure to interpolate between fully online and semi-online training.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Online training: Bootstrapping by conditioning on positive feedback (Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Online training: Bootstrapping by conditioning on positive feedback ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>)</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
            Initialize <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}=\theta_{\textrm{off}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}=\theta_{\textrm{off}}</annotation></semantics></math> from Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.1 Offline training: Initializing feedback-conditional policy ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, user-desired feedback <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{user}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{user}}({\bm{c}}^{+}|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, environment <math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\textrm{env}}({\bm{c}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>, instruct. corpus <math id="alg2.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, training rounds <math id="alg2.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, steps per round <math id="alg2.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="alg2.l1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, optimizer <math id="alg2.l1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
            The online-bootstrapped parameters <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg2.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}\leftarrow\theta_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}\leftarrow\theta_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg2.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for all</span> instructions <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\sim\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\sim\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> sampled in this round <span id="alg2.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              
               <span id="alg2.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rollout</span> <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="alg2.l6.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="17.24" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-4.84px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 91.39 17.24" width="91.39"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,17.24) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 13.09 C 0 15.39 1.86 17.24 4.15 17.24 L 87.24 17.24 C 89.53 17.24 91.39 15.39 91.39 13.09 L 91.39 4.15 C 91.39 1.86 89.53 0 87.24 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#ADD8E6;" fill="#ADD8E6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 13.09 C 0 15.39 1.86 17.24 4.15 17.24 L 87.24 17.24 C 89.53 17.24 91.39 15.39 91.39 13.09 L 91.39 4.15 C 91.39 1.86 89.53 0 87.24 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 2.77 4.84)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.2em;--ltx-fo-height:0.8em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.48" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.02)" width="85.86"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="alg2.l6.pic1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{+}\sim p_{\textrm{user}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">user</mtext></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{+}\sim p_{\textrm{user}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>, <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="alg2.l6.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="17.24" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-4.84px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 102.35 17.24" width="102.35"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,17.24) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 13.09 C 0 15.39 1.86 17.24 4.15 17.24 L 98.2 17.24 C 100.49 17.24 102.35 15.39 102.35 13.09 L 102.35 4.15 C 102.35 1.86 100.49 0 98.2 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#ADD8E6;" fill="#ADD8E6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 13.09 C 0 15.39 1.86 17.24 4.15 17.24 L 98.2 17.24 C 100.49 17.24 102.35 15.39 102.35 13.09 L 102.35 4.15 C 102.35 1.86 100.49 0 98.2 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 2.77 4.84)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:7em;--ltx-fo-height:0.8em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.48" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.02)" width="96.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="alg2.l6.pic2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{o}}\sim\pi_{\theta_{t-1}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{o}}\sim\pi_{\theta_{t-1}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> then obtain fresh critique <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{c}}\sim p_{\textrm{env}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>env</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}\sim p_{\textrm{env}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}},{\bm{o}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              
               <span id="alg2.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Push</span> <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},{\bm{o}},{\bm{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},{\bm{o}},{\bm{c}})</annotation></semantics></math> to buffer <math id="alg2.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{\textrm{on}}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mtext>on</mtext><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{\textrm{on}}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l7.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><span id="alg2.l7.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">// <math id="alg2.l7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">𝒄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> is usually different (at least linguistically) from <math id="alg2.l7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">𝒄</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
  </span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg2.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective:</span> <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="alg2.l8.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="19.23" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-7.47px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 188.2 19.23" width="188.2"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,19.23) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 15.08 C 0 17.37 1.86 19.23 4.15 19.23 L 184.05 19.23 C 186.35 19.23 188.2 17.37 188.2 15.08 L 188.2 4.15 C 188.2 1.86 186.35 0 184.05 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#ADD8E6;" fill="#ADD8E6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.15 L 0 15.08 C 0 17.37 1.86 19.23 4.15 19.23 L 184.05 19.23 C 186.35 19.23 188.2 17.37 188.2 15.08 L 188.2 4.15 C 188.2 1.86 186.35 0 184.05 0 L 4.15 0 C 1.86 0 0 1.86 0 4.15 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 2.77 7.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.33em;--ltx-fo-height:0em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="0" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)" width="4.61"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="alg2.l8.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">​<math id="alg2.l8.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\theta_{t}}\!\mathbb{E}_{({\bm{x}},{\bm{o}},{\bm{c}})\sim\mathcal{B}_{\textrm{on}}^{t}}\!\left[\log\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">max</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℬ</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">on</mtext><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒐</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒄</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\theta_{t}}\!\mathbb{E}_{({\bm{x}},{\bm{o}},{\bm{c}})\sim\mathcal{B}_{\textrm{on}}^{t}}\!\left[\log\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}|{\bm{x}},{\bm{c}})\right]</annotation></semantics></math>​</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> <span id="alg2.l8.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><span id="alg2.l8.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">// Taking expectation over <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\!\sim\!\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" mathcolor="#808080">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" lspace="0.108em" mathcolor="#808080" rspace="0.108em">∼</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#808080">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\!\sim\!\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.2 Online training: Bootstrapping by conditioning on positive feedback ‣ 2 Learning directly from verbal feedback ‣ Language Models Can Learn fromVerbal Feedback Without Scalar Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>)</span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg2.l9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg2.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
              
              
               Sample <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{o}}_{i},{\bm{c}}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{B}\sim\mathcal{B}_{\textrm{on}}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒄</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mtext>on</mtext><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{o}}_{i},{\bm{c}}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{B}\sim\mathcal{B}_{\textrm{on}}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>;  <math id="alg2.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}\leftarrow\mathcal{O}\mathrm{.step}\big(\theta_{t},\nabla_{\theta_{t}}\tfrac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\log\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}_{i}|{\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{c}}_{i})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>step</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒐</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒄</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}\leftarrow\mathcal{O}\mathrm{.step}\big(\theta_{t},\nabla_{\theta_{t}}\tfrac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\log\pi_{\theta_{t}}({\bm{o}}_{i}|{\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{c}}_{i})\big)</annotation></semantics></math>
  
</div>
<div id="alg2.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
              
              
